### B. The Object Example (Behavioral Difference)

**Code Demonstration:**
```java
class Random {
    int x;
    int y;
    Random(int x, int y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }
}

class Demo2 {
    public static void add10(Random r) {
        r.x = r.x + 10;
        r.y = r.y + 10;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Random r1 = new Random(4, 5);
        System.out.println(r1.x + ", " + r1.y); // Prints: 4, 5
        add10(r1);
        System.out.println(r1.x + ", " + r1.y); // Prints: 14, 15 (Values Changed!)
    }
}
```

**Teacher's Observation:**
* Unlike primitives, passing an object reference copies the **reference/address value** itself (Call by Value of the reference). 
* Both the original reference (`r1`) and the parameter reference (`r`) point to the **exact same object in the Heap**. 
* Therefore, modifications made through `r.x` and `r.y` directly mutate the heap object fields, which are visible when `r1.x` and `r1.y` are accessed afterward.


