### Non-Primitives in Method Signatures
- Methods can accept non-primitive data types as parameters (e.g., accepting a reference variable `R`).
- Methods can also **return** non-primitive data types (objects). For example:
  ```java
  Random add10(Random R) {
      // modifications...
      return R; // Valid return type of a non-primitive object
  }
  ```

### Constructors Accepting Object References
- You can define a constructor that accepts an object reference of the same class type as an argument to copy values:
  ```java
  Random(Random R) {
      this.x = R.x;
      this.y = R.y;
  }
  ```
- **Usage Example:**
  ```java
  Random R1 = new Random(4, 5);
  Random R2 = new Random(R1); // Passes R1 reference to the constructor
  ```

