### A. Default Constructor (Provided by Java)
- If a programmer **does not** explicitly write any constructor inside a class, the Java compiler automatically inserts a hidden, empty **Default Constructor** behind the scenes during compilation.
- **Syntax of the compiler-generated default constructor:**
  ```java
  Student() {
      // Empty body; leaves instance variables with their standard default values (0, null, etc.)
  }
  ```

### B. User-Defined Default / Non-Parameterised Constructor
- A constructor written explicitly by the programmer with **no parameters** to hardcode default initialization values.
- **Example:**
  ```java
  class Student {
      String name;
      int age;
      int rollNumber;
      String college;

      // User-defined constructor
      Student() {
          name = "Aditya";
          age = 28;
          rollNumber = 1001;
          college = "IIT Guwahati";
      }
  }
  ```

### Syntax and Implementation
A parameterized constructor is defined similarly to a normal function/method inside the class, accepting parameters that correspond to the object's instance variables.

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
    int rollNumber;
    int college; // or String college based on context

    // Parameterized Constructor
    Student(String n, int a, int rn, int c) {
        name = n;
        age = a;
        rollNumber = rn;
        college = c;
    }
}
```

### Example: Chaining Multiple Overloaded Constructors to a Master Constructor
If you have multiple constructors taking 0, 1, 2, 3, or 4 arguments, you can chain the smaller constructors to the largest, most comprehensive constructor:

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
    int rollNumber;
    String college;

    // 1. Master Constructor (Handles all 4 parameters)
    Student(String name, int age, int rollNumber, String college) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        this.rollNumber = rollNumber;
        this.college = college;
    }

    // 2. Default Constructor chained to the Master Constructor with default/unknown values
    Student() {
        this("Unnown", 0, 0, "Unnown"); 
    }

    // 3. One-parameter Constructor chained to the Master Constructor
    Student(String name) {
        this(name, 0, 0, "Unnown");
    }

    // 4. Two-parameter Constructor chained
    Student(String name, int age) {
        this(name, age, 0, "Unnown");
    }
}
```

