### Complete Code Implementation Example (From Lecture)

Below is the complete implementation tying together inheritance, constructors, and the `super` keyword as written by the instructor:

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
    int rollNumber;

    // Default Constructor
    Student() {
    }

    // Parameterized Constructor
    Student(String name, int age, int rollNumber) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        this.rollNumber = rollNumber;
    }

    void print() {
        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(age);
        System.out.println(rollNumber);
    }
}

class EngineeringStudent extends Student {
    String college;

    // Constructor using super to initialize parent fields
    EngineeringStudent(String name, int age, int rollNumber, String college) {
        super(name, age, rollNumber); // Calls Student's parameterized constructor
        this.college = college;
    }

    void print() {
        super.print(); // Calls Student's print method (prints name, age, rollNumber)
        System.out.println(college); // Prints college details
    }
}

// In Main Method execution:
// EngineeringStudent es2 = new EngineeringStudent("Aditya", 28, 1001, "IIT G");
// es2.print();
// Output: Aditya, 28, 1001, IIT G
```

### Why is it called "Run-Time" Polymorphism?
* **Object creation is dynamic:** Objects are created in memory at runtime using the `new` keyword. The specific object being instantiated can be decided conditionally at runtime based on inputs or application state.
* **Binding happens at runtime:** The compiler does not know in advance which version of the `run()` method will be executed. It depends entirely on which object is allocated in memory during execution.

**Dynamic Condition Example:**
```java
Animal a;
if (type.equals("duck")) {
    a = new Duck();
} else if (type.equals("human")) {
    a = new Human();
} else {
    a = new Dog();
}
a.run(); // Calls the respective run() method based on runtime evaluation
```

