### A. Static Methods
* **Rule:** Static methods **do not** get overridden.
* **Reasoning:** Static methods belong to the **class**, not to the object. 
* **Binding Behavior:** When a parent class reference (`a`) points to a child class object (`B`), calling a static method (`A.fun()`) will invoke the method belonging to the reference's class type (`A`), completely ignoring the object type (`B`).

**Code Example:**
```java
class A {
    static void fun() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}

class B extends A {
    static void fun() {
        System.out.println("Bye");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        A a = new B();
        a.fun(); // Output will be "Hello" because fun() is static and binds to class A
    }
}
```

## 4. Fields (Variables) and Polymorphism

* **Rule:** Fields or variables **do not** participate in polymorphism. They are **not** overridden.
* **Behavior:** Variable resolution depends strictly on the **reference type**, not on the object type in memory.

**Code Example:**
```java
class A {
    int x = 10;
}

class B extends A {
    int x = 20;
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        A a = new B();
        System.out.println(a.x); // Output is 10 (prints parent variable because reference type is A)

        B b = new B();
        System.out.println(b.x); // Output is 20 (prints child variable because reference type is B)
    }
}
```

* **Workaround to achieve polymorphic behavior for data:** If you need polymorphic value resolution, wrap the variable inside a method (e.g., `getV()`) and override that method in subclasses. Methods participate in polymorphism; variables do not.

