## 4. Deep-Dive: Static Nested Classes

A **Static Nested Class** is an inner class declared with the `static` keyword inside the body of an outer class.

```java
class Outer {
    // Static Nested Class
    static class Inner {
        // Class body
    }
}
```

### B. Instantiation Mechanics
Because a static nested class is not tied to an instance of the outer class, **you do NOT need to instantiate the outer class first** to create an object of the static nested class.

#### Qualified Class Name
The fully qualified type name of the nested class is:
`OuterClassName.InnerClassName`

#### Instantiation Syntax
```java
Outer.Inner innerObj = new Outer.Inner();
```

#### Step-by-Step Code Example
```java
public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Instantiating static nested class WITHOUT creating an Outer instance
        Outer.Inner inner = new Outer.Inner();

        // Calling an instance method on the nested object
        inner.fun();
    }
}

class Outer {
    static class Inner {
        void fun() {
            System.out.println("Hello");
        }
    }
}
```

---

### C. Scope & Access Rules

#### 1. Access to Outer Static Members (Direct Access Allowed)
A static nested class can directly access all `static` members (fields and methods) of the outer class, including `private static` members.

```java
class Outer {
    private static int x = 4; // Private static member

    static class Inner {
        void printStaticMember() {
            // Direct access to private static field 'x'
            System.out.println(x); // Prints: 4
        }
    }
}
```

#### 2. Access to Outer Non-Static Members (Direct Access PROHIBITED)
A static nested class **CANNOT** directly access non-static (instance) fields or methods of the outer class.

```java
class Outer {
    int y = 0; // Non-static instance variable

    static class Inner {
        void display() {
            // COMPILER ERROR: Cannot make a static reference to the non-static field y
            // System.out.println(y); 
        }
    }
}
```

##### Theoretical Reason for Restriction:
* Non-static fields (like `int y`) belong to a specific instance of `Outer`.
* A static nested class object can be created when **zero** instances of `Outer` exist in memory.
* If the static nested class were allowed to reference `y`, Java would not know *which* `Outer` instance's `y` field to access.

---

### D. Workarounds for Accessing Non-Static Members

If a static nested class *must* access non-static members of the outer class, an explicit instance reference to `Outer` must be provided.

#### Approach 1: Pass Outer Reference via Method Parameter
```java
class Outer {
    int y = 10; // Non-static member

    static class Inner {
        void fun(Outer outerRef) {
            // Access y via explicit outer reference
            System.out.println(outerRef.y);
        }
    }
}

// Usage:
Outer outerObj = new Outer();
Outer.Inner innerObj = new Outer.Inner();
innerObj.fun(outerObj);
```

#### Approach 2: Pass Outer Reference via Constructor
```java
class Outer {
    int y = 20;

    static class Inner {
        Outer outer;

        // Constructor accepting explicit reference to outer class
        Inner(Outer outer) {
            this.outer = outer;
        }

        void fun() {
            // Access instance member using stored outer reference
            System.out.println(this.outer.y);
        }
    }
}

// Usage:
Outer outerObj = new Outer();
Outer.Inner innerObj = new Outer.Inner(outerObj);
innerObj.fun();
```

