### 4.2 Default Implementation
If a class does not override `toString()`, calling `.toString()` executes the default implementation inherited from the `Object` class.

* **Default Return Format:** `ClassName@HexadecimalCode`
* **Hexadecimal Code:** Base-16 unique identifier value corresponding to the instance identity.

```java
Student s1 = new Student();
System.out.println(s1.toString()); 
// Output format: Student@15db9742 (Class Name + '@' + Hexadecimal Hash Code)
```

---

### 4.3 Overriding `toString()`
To customize how an object is displayed as text, override the `toString()` method in your class using the `@Override` annotation.

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + ", " + age;
    }
}
```

#### Code Execution Behavior:
```java
public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student();
        s1.name = "Aditya";
        s1.age = 28;

        // Explicit call
        System.out.println(s1.toString()); // Output: Aditya, 28

        // Implicit call
        System.out.println(s1); // Output: Aditya, 28
    }
}
```

> **Important Observation:** Passing an object directly to `System.out.println(s1)` automatically calls `s1.toString()` under the hood. Writing `.toString()` explicitly inside `println()` is optional.

