### Pseudo Code Structure for Bubble Sort
```c
// Outer loop for passes (Total passes = n - 1)
for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    // Inner loop for adjacent comparisons
    for (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {
        // Check if adjacent elements are out of order
        if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
            // Swapping logic using a temporary variable
            int temp = a[j];
            a[j] = a[j + 1];
            a[j + 1] = temp;
        }
    }
}
```
* **Swapping Mechanism Explained:**
  * Store A[j] inside a `temp` variable.
  * Move A[j+1] into A[j].
  * Move `temp` into A[j+1].

### 1.3 Pseudocode / Algorithmic Flow
```text
// Loop from n - 1 down to 0
for i = n - 1 down to 0:
    val = A[i]
    count[val] = count[val] - 1
    B[count[val]] = val

// Copy elements from auxiliary array B back to original array A
for i = 0 to n - 1:
    A[i] = B[i]
```

> **Reason for Decrementing**: Decrementing is required because `count` represents both cumulative counts and index positions. Decrementing ensures that duplicate values occupy adjacent preceding index positions without overwriting.

