### **1. Pass by Value (Call by Value)**
* **Mechanism:** A distinct copy of the variables is passed into the called function. Any modifications made to formal parameters inside the function affect **only** the local copies and do **not** reflect on the actual parameters in the calling function.
* **Example Code Logic:**
  ```cpp
  void increment(int a, int b) {
      a++; // Modifies local copy only
      b++;
  }
  int main() {
      int a = 2, b = 3;
      increment(a, b);
      // Printing a and b here will still output 2 and 3!
  }
  ```

### **2. Pass by Reference (Call by Reference)**
* **Mechanism:** The memory address (reference) of the variable is passed using the address-of operator (`&`). Any modifications made inside the called function directly alter the original values in the calling function.
* **Example Code Logic:**
  ```cpp
  void increment(int &a, int &b) {
      a++; // Modifies original memory location
      b++;
  }
  int main() {
      int a = 2, b = 3;
      increment(a, b);
      // Printing a and b here will output 3 and 4!
  }
  ```

